---
// src/layouts/Shell.astro
// localchune — MIT licensed. See LICENSE.
// NOTE: the distributed combination is AGPL-3.0 because the analysis
// worker includes Essentia. LICENSE explains why.
//
// The shell owns four things beyond the frame: the <ClientRouter />, the
// persisted player div, the persisted upload chip, and the one site script.
// The router swaps page bodies AROUND the transition:persist nodes, which is
// what lets playback survive filter submits, sort clicks, pagination and
// detail navigation — butternutcrack needed 345 lines and a store for the
// same property.
import { ClientRouter } from 'astro:transitions'
import AppNav from '../components/AppNav.astro'
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro'
import SourceLink from '../components/SourceLink.astro'
import StorageChip from '../components/StorageChip.astro'
import UploadChip from '../components/UploadChip'
import { LIKE_ICON } from '../lib/track-format'
import '../styles/global.css'

// Task 8: `center` is for the app's "single card" pages (currently just
// /welcome — /login has no Shell at all, see its own body.loginpage rule
// in global.css). It adds body.centerpage, which vertically AND
// horizontally centers <main>'s content between AppNav and the footer —
// see global.css's `body.centerpage main` rule for the mechanics.
interface Props { title: string; center?: boolean }
const { title, center = false } = Astro.props
---
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  {/* viewport-fit=cover is the PREREQUISITE for env(safe-area-inset-*).
      Without it those functions evaluate to 0 on every device, so every
      safe-area rule in global.css silently does nothing and the seek
      slider keeps sitting inside the iOS home-indicator gesture zone. */}
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover" />
  <title>{title}</title>
  <ClientRouter />
</head>
<body class={center ? 'centerpage' : undefined}>
  <AppNav />
  <main><slot /></main>
  <footer class="appfoot">
    <StorageChip />
    <SourceLink />
  </footer>
  {/* Task 3: native `controls` renders inconsistently on mobile and hides
      the seek affordance entirely on some phones. The <audio> element
      stays as the playback engine (no `controls` attr, so it draws no
      box of its own) and this custom row — play/pause, a touch-friendly
      range seek, elapsed/total — replaces it. Everything here lives
      inside the SAME transition:persist="player" node as before, so it
      survives a ClientRouter navigation exactly like the old markup did;
      see src/scripts/site.ts for the wiring. */}
  <div class="playerbar" transition:persist="player">
    {/* M6b Task 7 — the visible queue. INERT MARKUP ONLY: site.ts fills both
        containers and builds every control inside them. That is not laziness,
        it is the bundle rule — this layout mounts on every page, so an island
        here would ship the engine, the four strategies and the candidate
        client to /login. Nothing in this file imports a queue module at all,
        and src/layouts/shell-bundle.test.ts fails the build if that changes.

        It lives INSIDE the persisted player node so a ClientRouter navigation
        moves it rather than recreating it.

        THAT REASON CHANGED ON 2026-08-08 AND THE COMMENT HAS TO CHANGE WITH
        IT. It used to read "otherwise the drawer would close itself on every
        filter submit and every sort click, which is exactly the navigation a
        member does while building a queue" — and that stopped being true the
        moment the owner asked for a scrim ("the background page must not be
        usable as with any queues"). A member cannot click a filter or a sort
        with the drawer open any more; the page behind it is inert.

        The persistence still earns its place, for three reasons that do not
        depend on that one:
          · a navigation that happens ANYWAY — the member closes the drawer,
            clicks through, and the queue, the scroll position of its list
            and the loaded drag module are all still there;
          · a PROGRAMMATIC soft nav (a redirect, a form that routes) with the
            drawer open must not destroy it mid-gesture;
          · every element reference site.ts captured once at module load
            stays valid, because <script src> modules evaluate once per
            document and are never re-run on a swap. Recreating this node
            would silently drop every listener bound to it.
        site.ts re-applies the scrim, the `inert` flags and the scroll lock
        on astro:after-swap, because those live on <body>, which IS
        replaced.

        Zero <form> elements, and that is the honest position rather than a
        dodge: the queue is client state, so there is no server resource for a
        queue operation to POST to. Every control is <button type="button">,
        which is also why astro-forms.test.ts stays green — this milestone
        adds no POST form anywhere. */}
    <div id="queue-drawer" class="queuedrawer" hidden aria-label="Playback queue">
      {/* THE GRAB HANDLE — OWNER, 2026-08-07: "the current queue drawer cant
          be closed by dragging it down and that feels like a bummer… make it
          like the drawer component of add to crate". The bar he had earlier
          deferred ("shutter like icon on top of queue… keep that for last")
          is that drawer's, and this is the same class rendering the same
          36x4 bar with the same 44px hit box from its ::after — one rule in
          global.css, not a copy.

          MARKUP RATHER THAN site.ts, unlike every other control in this
          drawer. The bundle rule that keeps the engine out of /login is
          about IMPORTS, and a bare button imports nothing; putting it here
          means it exists at first paint and, more importantly, that
          renderDrawer() cannot destroy it — that function rebuilds
          #queue-methods and #queue-sections on every queue change, and a
          handle inside either would lose its listener several times a
          minute.

          It ships WITHOUT `hidden`, which every other JS-only control in
          this file carries, and the exception is structural rather than an
          oversight: its parent is the drawer, the drawer ships `hidden`, and
          only site.ts can unhide it. There is no no-JS render in which this
          button is reachable.

          It is a real <button>, so the drag has a keyboard and screen-reader
          equivalent: tap or Enter closes the drawer, which is the same
          promise the sheet's handle makes. */}
      <button type="button" id="queue-grab" class="grabhandle" aria-label="Close the queue"></button>
      <div class="queuedrawer-head">
        <div id="queue-methods" class="queuemethods" role="group" aria-label="Autoplay method"></div>
        <button type="button" id="queue-clear" class="btn-danger">CLEAR</button>
      </div>
      <div id="queue-sections" class="queuesections"></div>
    </div>
    {/* THE STATUS STRIP — OWNER, 2026-08-08, and it is a BUG FIX with a
        shape rather than a feature.

        Reported with a screenshot: the bar read "already in It just goes
        and goes" and never went away, with the track title and its link
        GONE — so the track page could not be reached from the bar at all.
        A second instance: "artist — song name — 21 of 100 pool", equally
        permanent.

        The cause was that #player-label below was BOTH the now-playing
        identity AND the one aria-live status region. A status write did
        `label.textContent = text`, which detaches every child including the
        title anchor, and NOTHING ever put it back. The comment that used to
        live here called those messages "transient"; they never were.

        The owner's answer, and it is the better one: give the message its
        own strip above the bar, let it leave on its own, and never let it
        touch the title. The bug is now IMPOSSIBLE rather than fixed —
        there is no code path from a status message to the identity, and
        src/lib/player-label-single-source.test.ts fails the build if one
        appears.

        THIS IS THE APP'S ONE LIVE REGION NOW. aria-live moved OFF
        #player-label and onto this strip, which is what survive-list #12
        was always protecting: one region, so two announcements cannot race.
        `role="status"` is the same promise spelled for older assistive
        tech; the two together are redundant and deliberately so.

        INERT MARKUP, hidden, and empty: site.ts writes the text, shows it,
        and hides it again. It ships inside the persisted player node so a
        soft navigation moves it like everything else here, and it is
        ABSOLUTELY positioned above the bar so that showing a message
        NEVER changes the bar's height — which would move the queue
        drawer, the upload chip and the page's own bottom padding, all of
        which are derived from --bar-h. */}
    <div id="player-status" class="playerstatus" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden><span id="player-status-text" class="playerstatus-text"></span></div>
    {/* THE TITLE LINK AND THE ARTIST LINE — IDENTITY ONLY.

        The track NAME is the anchor below rather than a text node, so a
        member can reach the track page from whatever page they are on
        without losing playback (that is the whole point of the persisted
        bar; a ClientRouter soft-nav moves this node instead of recreating
        it).

        The anchor ships `hidden` and with NO href: an empty href resolves
        to the current page, which is a dead link on every page but one.
        site.ts sets both together — §"no dead link".

        TWO LINES, NOT ONE JOINED STRING — owner-confirmed layout,
        2026-08-06. The title is the link; the artist is its own smaller,
        muted line below it, with no em-dash join. Both ellipsize
        independently, which is what a 6rem flex basis and one nowrap span
        could never do: a joined "Artist — Title" truncated to the artist
        and three characters of the title, which is the half a member does
        not need.

        The two lines are written from the QUEUE ENTRY's own display_title
        and display_artist — the same pair Media Session already sends to
        the lock screen. Nothing parses the joined string back apart; a
        title containing an em dash would make that a guess, and the
        correct fields were there all along.

        EACH LINE WRAPS ITS TEXT IN A SPAN — owner, 2026-08-08: "also can
        the track name also have a marquee." The anchor is the clipping box
        and keeps its full width, so the whole line stays tappable and the
        link's hit area is exactly what it was; the span inside is the part
        that moves. site.ts measures the span against the anchor and only
        animates the line that actually overflows.

        aria-live is NOT here any more — see the strip above. */}
    <span id="player-label" class="nowplaying"><a id="player-link" class="nowplayinglink" hidden><span class="marquee-text"></span></a><a id="player-artist" class="nowplayingartist" hidden><span class="marquee-text"></span></a></span>
    {/* THE ♥, for whatever is playing.
        A REAL POST FORM in the exact shape TrackRow.astro's pool cell and
        track/[id].astro's .signals block already use — form.likeform >
        button.likebtn[data-file-id] > .likeglyph + .likecount — so site.ts's
        ONE document-level submit delegation serves this button with no new
        like logic at all. site.ts rewrites `action` and `data-file-id` as
        the current track changes.

        data-astro-reload for the reason every POST form in this repo carries
        it (see CLAUDE.md): if the delegation ever declines the submit, the
        browser must send a genuine x-www-form-urlencoded POST, which is the
        only encoding /api/track/[id]/like's plain-form branch understands.

        Hidden AND disabled in the markup, unlike the two buttons below which
        are only hidden. Hidden alone would be enough to make it unreachable,
        but this is the one control here that can POST, and it ships with no
        action — so the disabled attribute is the belt to that braces. site.ts
        clears both only once it has a real track id to point at. */}
    <form id="player-like" method="post" class="likeform playerlike" hidden data-astro-reload>
      <button type="submit" class="likebtn" data-file-id="" aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Like" disabled
      ><span class="likeglyph"><Icon name={LIKE_ICON.name} size={LIKE_ICON.size} /></span> <span class="likecount">0</span></button>
    </form>
    {/* THE CRATE, IMMEDIATELY BESIDE THE ♥ — owner, 2026-08-07: "add the add
        to crate button in playback right alongside the like button". Same
        row, same size, same treatment, adjacent: the two together are the
        bar's "what do I do with this track" group, and the transport row
        below is "what is it doing". Row 1 is name-stack · ♥ · crate.

        NOT a `<details class="cratepick">` like a row's picker, and the
        reason is geometry rather than taste: `.cratepick-menu` opens
        DOWNWARD out of a `position: relative` summary, and this control is
        fixed to the bottom edge of the viewport — the menu would open
        off-screen on every device. It opens the SHARED picker sheet
        instead, which is the same component the row ⋮ already opens
        (§5.2 names the crate picker as one of the sheet's four jobs), so
        there is no second picker and no second dismissal behaviour.

        Hidden in the markup like #queue-toggle and #player-next, for the
        same reason: with no JS there is no sheet to open, and a control
        that does nothing must not be visible. site.ts unhides it in
        setPlayerCrate() the moment a track id exists — the same call that
        arms the ♥, so the two can never disagree about whether the bar
        knows what is playing.

        aria-haspopup="dialog" rather than "menu": it opens a sheet, which
        is the same promise RowMenu.astro makes. */}
    <button
      id="player-crate"
      type="button"
      class="playercrate"
      hidden
      aria-haspopup="dialog"
      aria-label="Add to a crate"
    ><Icon name="crate" size={20} /></button>
    {/* Hidden until site.ts unhides it: with no JS there is no queue for it
        to open, and a control that does nothing must not be visible. */}
    <button
      id="queue-toggle"
      type="button"
      class="queuetoggle btn-secondary"
      hidden
      aria-expanded="false"
      aria-controls="queue-drawer"
    ><Icon name="queue" size={16} /> QUEUE</button>
    {/* OWNER, 2026-08-07: no text-glyph control anywhere. `⏸` and `⏭` carry
        emoji presentation, so iOS painted the transport as colour emoji in
        the middle of a monochrome bar. These are the set's own `play`,
        `pause` and `next`; site.ts swaps the first between play and pause and
        never touches the other. The accessible name stays on the button —
        every glyph in the set is aria-hidden. */}
    <button id="player-toggle" type="button" class="playertoggle" aria-label="Play"><Icon name="play" size={20} /></button>
    {/* The queue had no on-page skip control at all: the only way to reach the
        next track was the lock screen, which is unreachable on a desktop and
        was itself inert on a resumed session. Hidden until site.ts unhides it,
        for the same reason #queue-toggle is — without JS there is no queue to
        advance through, and a control that does nothing must not be visible. */}
    <button id="player-next" type="button" class="playertoggle playernext" hidden aria-label="Next track"><Icon name="next" size={20} /></button>
    <input id="player-seek" type="range" class="playerseek" min="0" max="0" step="1" value="0" aria-label="Seek" />
    <span id="player-time" class="playertime num">0:00 / --:--</span>
    <audio id="player-audio" preload="none"></audio>
  </div>
  {/* UX.12: browse while uploading. The engine itself is a module singleton
      (src/lib/upload-engine.ts) and survives a soft navigation with or
      without this island — but the CHIP has to render on every page and must
      not remount on each one, so it persists exactly like the player above.
      Mounted AFTER .playerbar so that if the two ever overlap, the chip wins
      the paint order without needing a z-index. client:load rather than
      client:idle: a member returning to a tab mid-batch should see the chip
      immediately, not after the main thread goes quiet. */}
  <UploadChip client:load transition:persist="uploadchip" />
  <script src="../scripts/site.ts"></script>
</body>
</html>
